# Written by "fyne package" into the project root before it builds, then
# deleted when it is done. While the file is there, "git status --porcelain"
# is not empty -- and that is exactly what cmd/go reads to decide vcs.modified.
# Every release up to v0.2.3 therefore announced itself as ", modified", which
# is the flag meant to mean the opposite (R-06). Ignoring the file keeps the
# tree reading clean for the one moment that matters.
fyne_metadata_init.go

# Same story on Windows, where the tool writes two more files into the root.
# fyne.syso is the resource holding the icon and the version info, and it has
# to exist before the link step, so it is present at exactly the wrong moment;
# superview.exe is the intermediate binary, named after the module. Both were
# found by watching "git status --porcelain" in a loop while the Windows job
# packaged -- neither is visible once the tool has finished.
*.syso
superview.exe

# The Linux half of the same story, which #43 did not cover: "fyne package"
# leaves the intermediate binary named after the module -- "superview", with no
# extension -- and a "tmp-pkg/" staging directory in the project root. Watching
# "git status --porcelain" through a Linux packaging run shows all three of
# "superview", "tmp-pkg/" and the archive as untracked while the tool works.
# Only "superview.exe" was ignored, so the Linux side had the asymmetry that
# R-06 was about. The guard on the packaged binary in release.yml would have
# caught it at the next release; ignoring these means it does not have to.
superview
tmp-pkg/

# "fyne package" leaves its archive in the working directory.
superview-gui-*.tar.xz
superview-gui-*.zip
